INT. HOLLOWAY HOUSE - KITCHEN - NIGHT

The lights died at nine and the whole street went dark with them, and now a single candle gutters on the formica table while Sam Holloway, who has not slept properly since the summer of 1974, pours two fingers of rye into a chipped mug, and Jules, Sam's partner at the precinct, leans against the humming, silent fridge with a flashlight pointed at the floor and a cigarette burning down between two nicotine-stained fingers.

SAM (V.O.)
Rule one of a blackout. Nobody trusts anybody. Not even the guy who drove you here.

JULES
They cut the phone lines before the power. That's not weather, Sam.

SAM
(drinking)
I know what it is.

SAM (V.O.)
I knew the day I took the badge that every kitchen in this city would end up like this one. Quiet. Waiting for the other shoe.

JULES
(low)
They're across the street. I counted two. Maybe three.

SAM
They have a way of looking at the window. Same way my father looked at it. Like it owed him money.

Jules clicks the flashlight off. The candle jumps. A car idles somewhere outside, headlights sweeping the curtains once, twice, and moving on.

JULES
So we sit here and drink?

SAM
(setting the mug down)
We sit here and listen.

SAM (V.O.)
The truth about waiting is that it never ends. It just changes clothes.
